>>
endobj
106 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 85 0 R
/T (The efficient market theory [1][2] suggests that asset prices already reflect all available information at all times, making it impossible for any individual or institution to always beat the market, but this theory doesn't always hold because of some persistent anomalies [3] that challenge market efficiency, one of which is the irrationality and herd mentality among retail participants of the market [6] that often deviates the market from its fundamental value. The anomaly we will be focusing on here is the overnight return anomaly [4], which is the most persistent and widely documented of all. Evidence from several developed and emerging markets [12] [13] suggests that a disproportionately large share of long-term equity returns is generated during non-trading hours, while intraday returns contribute relatively little to overall returns. This finding raises an important question regarding the degree of market efficiency. The existing explanation proposes that the existence of this anomaly primarily comes from information releases [5] such as earnings announcements, macroeconomic events, and global market development that occur frequently beyond trading hours. In addition to this, order imbalances and unexecuted trades that persist beyond trading hours artificially inflate the price when the market reopens, potentially contributing to overnight price adjustments. While the overnight anomaly has garnered considerable attention in the international market, relatively few studies have been made on its exploitability within the Indian market. More importantly, much of the existing literature focuses on identifying the anomaly rather than evaluating whether it can be transformed into a profitable trading strategy after accounting for real-world implementation costs [9].)
/E (The efficient market theory [1][2] suggests that asset prices already reflect all available information at all times, making it impossible for any individual or institution to always beat the market, but this theory doesn't always hold because of some persistent anomalies [3] that challenge market efficiency, one of which is the irrationality and herd mentality among retail participants of the market [6] that often deviates the market from its fundamental value. The anomaly we will be focusing on here is the overnight return anomaly [4], which is the most persistent and widely documented of all. Evidence from several developed and emerging markets [12] [13] suggests that a disproportionately large share of long-term equity returns is generated during non-trading hours, while intraday returns contribute relatively little to overall returns. This finding raises an important question regarding the degree of market efficiency. The existing explanation proposes that the existence of this anomaly primarily comes from information releases [5] such as earnings announcements, macroeconomic events, and global market development that occur frequently beyond trading hours. In addition to this, order imbalances and unexecuted trades that persist beyond trading hours artificially inflate the price when the market reopens, potentially contributing to overnight price adjustments. While the overnight anomaly has garnered considerable attention in the international market, relatively few studies have been made on its exploitability within the Indian market. More importantly, much of the existing literature focuses on identifying the anomaly rather than evaluating whether it can be transformed into a profitable trading strategy after accounting for real-world implementation costs [9].)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [12]
>>
