/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 87 0 R
/T (These findings provide preliminary evidence of an overnight market anomaly [12] in the indian equity market, thus motivating further study of whether this can be economically exploited through index futures after accounting for transaction costs and market friction. Despite the extensive documentation of the overnight return anomaly, there is no universally accepted reason for its persistence. Existing literature suggests that this phenomenon is a combination of information asymmetry and behavioural bias. These reasons aren't mutually exclusive and could contribute together to the observed return during non-trading hours.  One of the most accepted reasons is the information arrival hypothesis [5], since markets operate within fixed hours. In contrast, information relevant to the asset prices, like earnings announcements and geopolitical developments, often takes place outside trading hours. Consequently, prices adjust to new information at the subsequent market open [14], leading to concentration of returns during the overnight period. Behavioural bias [6] also offers a potential explanation for this anomaly. Institutions often execute large orders before the market closes to rebalance portfolios, and retail participation is disproportionately high at market open as a reaction to the overnight information. Such systematic trading patterns can generate temporary price pressure, contributing to persistent differences between overnight and intraday returns. Although each of these explanations provides really detailed insight, no single theory completely accounts for the magnitude and persistence of the overnight return anomaly across different markets and time periods. While there exists a considerable amount of literature documenting the existence of this anomaly across equity markets [4][12][13], comparatively fewer studies examine whether this anomaly remains economically exploitable after accounting for realistic trading costs. A strategy may generate positive gross returns but could be unprofitable once implementation costs are considered. This study focuses on constructing and backtesting a systematic overnight strategy using NIFTY Index Futures from June 2020 to June 2026. Unlike prior studies focusing on gross return differentials, this research incorporates realistic transaction costs, statutory charges, and execution frictions to evaluate the economic viability of exploiting the overnight anomaly. The performance of this strategy is assessed relative to a buy-and-hold strategy benchmark using both absolute and risk-adjusted performance measures.)
/E (These findings provide preliminary evidence of an overnight market anomaly [12] in the indian equity market, thus motivating further study of whether this can be economically exploited through index futures after accounting for transaction costs and market friction. Despite the extensive documentation of the overnight return anomaly, there is no universally accepted reason for its persistence. Existing literature suggests that this phenomenon is a combination of information asymmetry and behavioural bias. These reasons aren't mutually exclusive and could contribute together to the observed return during non-trading hours.  One of the most accepted reasons is the information arrival hypothesis [5], since markets operate within fixed hours. In contrast, information relevant to the asset prices, like earnings announcements and geopolitical developments, often takes place outside trading hours. Consequently, prices adjust to new information at the subsequent market open [14], leading to concentration of returns during the overnight period. Behavioural bias [6] also offers a potential explanation for this anomaly. Institutions often execute large orders before the market closes to rebalance portfolios, and retail participation is disproportionately high at market open as a reaction to the overnight information. Such systematic trading patterns can generate temporary price pressure, contributing to persistent differences between overnight and intraday returns. Although each of these explanations provides really detailed insight, no single theory completely accounts for the magnitude and persistence of the overnight return anomaly across different markets and time periods. While there exists a considerable amount of literature documenting the existence of this anomaly across equity markets [4][12][13], comparatively fewer studies examine whether this anomaly remains economically exploitable after accounting for realistic trading costs. A strategy may generate positive gross returns but could be unprofitable once implementation costs are considered. This study focuses on constructing and backtesting a systematic overnight strategy using NIFTY Index Futures from June 2020 to June 2026. Unlike prior studies focusing on gross return differentials, this research incorporates realistic transaction costs, statutory charges, and execution frictions to evaluate the economic viability of exploiting the overnight anomaly. The performance of this strategy is assessed relative to a buy-and-hold strategy benchmark using both absolute and risk-adjusted performance measures.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
