632 0 obj
<<
/Title (The efficient market theory [1][2] suggests that asset prices already reflect all available information at all times, making it impossible for any individual or institution to always beat the market, but this theory doesn't always hold because of some persistent anomalies [3] that challenge market efficiency, one of which is the irrationality and herd mentality among retail participants of the market [6] that often deviates the market from its fundamental value. The anomaly we will be focusing on here is the overnight return anomaly [4], which is the most persistent and widely documented of all. Evidence from several developed and emerging markets [12] [13] suggests that a disproportionately large share of long-term equity returns is generated during non-trading hours, while intraday returns contribute relatively little to overall returns. This finding raises an important question regarding the degree of market efficiency. The existing explanation proposes that the existence of this anomaly primarily comes from information releases [5] such as earnings announcements, macroeconomic events, and global market development that occur frequently beyond trading hours. In addition to this, order imbalances and unexecuted trades that persist beyond trading hours artificially inflate the price when the market reopens, potentially contributing to overnight price adjustments. While the overnight anomaly has garnered considerable attention in the international market, relatively few studies have been made on its exploitability within the Indian market. More importantly, much of the existing literature focuses on identifying the anomaly rather than evaluating whether it can be transformed into a profitable trading strategy after accounting for real-world implementation costs [9].)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 586 0 R
/First 682 0 R
/Last 682 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 633 0 R
>>
endobj
633 0 obj
<<
/Title (A strategy that may seem statistically significant may prove to be unprofitable once brokerage charges, taxes, slippage, and other market frictions are considered. The primary objective of this study is to determine whether the excess return associated with overnight price movements remains economically meaningful after accounting for implementation costs [9]. By focusing on the index futures markets, rather than solely focusing on the return pattern, this paper contributes to the literature on market anomalies by bridging the gap between statistical evidence and practical investment application.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 586 0 R
/Prev 632 0 R
>>
endobj
634 0 obj
<<
/Title <FEFF00520028006400610069006C007900290020003D0020005200280069006E00740072006100640061007900290020002B0020005220921D6500200028006F007600650072006E00690067006800740029>
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 451 0 R
/Prev 587 0 R
/Next 683 0 R
>>
endobj
635 0 obj
<<
/Title (These findings provide preliminary evidence of an overnight market anomaly [12] in the indian equity market, thus motivating further study of whether this can be economically exploited through index futures after accounting for transaction costs and market friction. Despite the extensive documentation of the overnight return anomaly, there is no universally accepted reason for its persistence. Existing literature suggests that this phenomenon is a combination of information asymmetry and behavioural bias. These reasons aren't mutually exclusive and could contribute together to the observed return during non-trading hours.  One of the most accepted reasons is the information arrival hypothesis [5], since markets operate within fixed hours. In contrast, information relevant to the asset prices, like earnings announcements and geopolitical developments, often takes place outside trading hours. Consequently, prices adjust to new information at the subsequent market open [14], leading to concentration of returns during the overnight period. Behavioural bias [6] also offers a potential explanation for this anomaly. Institutions often execute large orders before the market closes to rebalance portfolios, and retail participation is disproportionately high at market open as a reaction to the overnight information. Such systematic trading patterns can generate temporary price pressure, contributing to persistent differences between overnight and intraday returns. Although each of these explanations provides really detailed insight, no single theory completely accounts for the magnitude and persistence of the overnight return anomaly across different markets and time periods. While there exists a considerable amount of literature documenting the existence of this anomaly across equity markets [4][12][13], comparatively fewer studies examine whether this anomaly remains economically exploitable after accounting for realistic trading costs. A strategy may generate positive gross returns but could be unprofitable once implementation costs are considered. This study focuses on constructing and backtesting a systematic overnight strategy using NIFTY Index Futures from June 2020 to June 2026. Unlike prior studies focusing on gross return differentials, this research incorporates realistic transaction costs, statutory charges, and execution frictions to evaluate the economic viability of exploiting the overnight anomaly. The performance of this strategy is assessed relative to a buy-and-hold strategy benchmark using both absolute and risk-adjusted performance measures.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 451 0 R
/Prev 684 0 R
/Next 588 0 R
>>
endobj
636 0 obj
<<
/Title (Having established the existence of this anomaly in the indian equity market, the subsequent analysis examines whether this can be transformed into a profitable and practically implementable investment strategy. A systematic overnight trading strategy is constructed using NIFTY Index futures, providing liquid and efficient exposure to the underlying index. This section outlines the framework employed to implement and execute this strategy. Covering the trading rules, portfolio construction methodology, transaction cost assumptions, and performance evaluation metrics presented in detail. The performance of the proposed strategy is compared to a passive buy-and-hold benchmark using both absolute and risk-adjusted performance measures)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 589 0 R
/Next 685 0 R
>>
endobj
